<h2>The question</h2>

<p>The conveyor's session reaper stops finished background sessions. On <code>main</code> it is one 488-line file,
<code>scripts/conveyor/session-reaper.mjs</code>, run by hand at hand-off. On the prototype branch
<code>lane/mechanical-dispatcher</code> it is split into a pure planner, an evidence module, a stop module and a
323-line facade, and the stop module carries the fix for
<a href="/backlog/3744-session-reaper-cannot-verify-a-stop-took-effect-and-re-stops/">#3744</a> (the reaper
re-stopped about 570 finished sessions per run). Decision
<a href="/backlog/3802-decision-graduate-the-session-reaper-planner-first-graduate/">#3802</a> asks how the
reaper reaches <code>main</code>, where its one blocking dependency goes, and what an unmarked session name means
there. The full working is in <a href="/reports/2026-09-21-session-reaper-graduation-grounding.md">the session
report</a>. How a slice is ported (any time, as a diff onto main's current file) is already ruled by
<a href="/backlog/3804-decision-how-the-prototype-branch-is-kept-up-to-date-with-ma/">#3804</a>.</p>

<h2>Finding 1: the closure is 13 new files, not 110</h2>

<p>The earlier count (218 files, 110 missing from or different from <code>main</code>) walked every static import
of the reaper. Re-run on the staging ref it reads 253 files, 139 identical to <code>main</code>, 51 different, 62
missing. But a file <code>main</code> already has does not have to be ported; it only has to export the names the
reaper asks for. Counting only the files <code>main</code> lacks, and checking every named import against
<code>main</code>'s copy (all satisfied):</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Piece</th><th>New files on main</th><th>Lines (staging ref)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Planner</td><td><code>session-reap-plan</code>, <code>session-verdicts</code>, <code>land-advance-tools</code></td><td>294 + 202 + 26</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Evidence</td><td><code>session-reap-evidence</code>, <code>session-verdicts-io</code></td><td>195 + 132</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Stop (carries #3744)</td><td><code>session-reap-stop</code></td><td>314</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Watchdog chain</td><td><code>driver-watchdog</code>, <code>driver-mode</code></td><td>890 + 145</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Facade (replaces main's monolith)</td><td>none (a rewrite of <code>session-reaper.mjs</code>)</td><td>325 (was 488)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>readFollowUps</code> if imported from land-advance</td><td>5 more: <code>land-advance-io</code>, <code>land-advance</code>, <code>land-advance-escalations</code>, <code>land-advance-repair</code>, <code>ci-heal-pr-dispatch</code></td><td>n/a</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Only one function forces the last row: <code>readFollowUps</code>, five lines over the run store, lives in
<code>land-advance-io.mjs</code>. Extracted to a leaf, the whole reaper is 8 new files plus the leaf.</p>

<h2>Finding 2: the planner slice stands alone, measured</h2>

<p>A copy of <code>origin/main</code> with only the planner's three files and the planner test added runs 36 of 36
planner tests green. The verdicts test does not: it imports the facade, <code>land-advance-escalations</code> and
<code>land-advance.mjs</code> (for <code>OWED_ACTIONS</code>), so that test is rewired or held back, not carried as
is.</p>

<h2>Finding 3: the stop module fixes nothing on main until the facade swap</h2>

<p>Nothing on <code>main</code> imports the new modules. Main's monolith keeps its own stop code, so the #3744 fix
takes effect only when the facade replaces the monolith. The earlier card said #3744 would close when the stop
module graduated; it closes at the last slice.</p>

<h2>Finding 4: the stop module answers a human gate on its own</h2>

<p>The prototype's stop module auto-answers <code>proceed</code> to the <code>authorize</code> step of the
<code>clear-stuck-session</code> operation, for any session whose <code>claude stop</code> threw. That step is a
person's by the operation's own header: the move is "recoverable (quarantined, never deleted), it is still
irreversible-ish in effect: the daemon is expected to drop the session from its listings once its job directory is
gone." Whether the gate may be narrowed is the open, unprepared decision
<a href="/backlog/3684-narrow-the-human-authorize-gate-in-clear-stuck-session-for-o/">#3684</a>, whose approved
direction is to collect a scorecard first. On this machine the operation has 15 recorded runs: 13 moved a job
directory, each after a human answer of 2.8 to 199 seconds, 1 was refused, 1 is pending. The repair fires only when a
stop throws, not for sessions whose stop "succeeded" but still list, so holding it back costs little.</p>

<h2>Finding 5: untagged names are normal on main and not yet gone elsewhere</h2>

<p><code>CONSTELLATION_REPOS.we.slugTag</code> is empty, so <code>mintSessionSlug</code> emits
<code>review-148</code>, with no marker, for a WE pull request; only other repos get <code>review-pa-148</code>. But
the prototype's review dispatch still mints <code>reviewSessionSlug(prNum)</code> with no repo, and four minters on
the staging ref still do (<code>ci-heal-pr-dispatch</code>, <code>review-dispatch-wrapper</code>,
<code>review-dispatch</code> at the evidence call, <code>dispatch-lane</code>). The live session listing on
2026-09-21: 885 rows, 455 with a PR-style name, 18 tagged, 437 untagged. Of the untagged, 363 started before the
marker code landed on main (2026-09-20 08:45 EDT), 13 between that commit and the first tagged session (12:17 EDT),
61 after. Three untagged sessions are not terminal, all from before the commit. Numbers do collide (WE has pull
requests 1 to about 2,300, so a plateau-app number in the 50 to 150 range has a WE twin), but the every-repo check
reaps only when every existing PR of that number is terminal, so a session whose real PR is open is kept.</p>

<h2>Prior art</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Strangler Fig (Fowler):</strong> "Like the fig, it begins with small additions, often new features,
    that are built on top of, yet separate to the legacy code base." and "Since these components are small, there
    isn't so much risk involved when we introduce the new software."</li>
  <li><strong>Branch By Abstraction (Fowler):</strong> a long change is done so the team can "release the system
    regularly while the change is still in-progress", with the system building and running "correctly at all
    times". Steps: build the new supplier behind the abstraction, switch a section of clients, repeat until all
    use the new one. The reaper's facade is the abstraction, the split modules the new supplier.</li>
  <li><strong>Parallel Change (Fowler):</strong> expand, migrate, contract. Landing the new modules beside the
    monolith is the expand step; the facade swap is the migrate step; nothing to contract because the swap
    replaces the old file.</li>
  <li><strong>Google engineering practices, small changes:</strong> "100 lines is usually a reasonable size for a
    CL, and 1000 lines is usually too large, but it's up to the judgment of your reviewer." Related tests ship with
    the code. The watchdog chain alone is 1,035 lines.</li>
  <li><strong>Terraform apply:</strong> <code>-auto-approve</code> "Skips interactive approval of the plan before
    applying", and the docs recommend using it in automation only after a plan has been performed and reviewed. The
    default asks; the unattended path is an explicit opt-in.</li>
  <li><strong>Kubernetes namespaces:</strong> "Names of resources need to be unique within a namespace, but not
    across namespaces", and an unqualified name resolves in the <code>default</code> namespace. A name with no
    scope is safe only where the default is defined and stable; a name minted before that rule needs a stated
    boundary, not a guess.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How the decision was reshaped</h2>

<p>The filed draft had three forks. Two skeptic passes and two fresh-context screens changed it as follows. The
slicing fork (planner first, one piece, or keep on the prototype) dissolved into a ratify: the epic already rules
that each increment is its own small pull request, and the only broken branch is keeping the reaper on the
prototype. The <code>readFollowUps</code> fork dissolved into a build note, because which file holds a five-line
reader is not a call a person needs to make. A new fork was added for the stop module's human-gate answer (default:
split it out and hold it for #3684; the close alternative is to graduate it opt-in and off by default on both
sides). The untagged-name fork kept its filed default, to check every repo for every untagged name, after an attack
that tried to replace it with a start-time cutover, and its sunset was rewritten from a fleet-state condition to a
test-pinned code condition. The cost of the whole reaper was corrected from 110 files to 13.</p>
